# ADR 0002: The plugin is a subprocess, not a library

- Status: accepted
- Date: {{ date }}

## Context

A plugin has to reach into whatever it integrates with - a mail client, a
corporate API, a device - and it has to be shipped and updated separately from
the application it extends.

Three shapes were available. A dynamic library loaded into the host: Rust has
no stable ABI, so every host release would break every plugin built against the
previous one. A sandboxed runtime such as WebAssembly: the integrations a
plugin exists for are exactly the ones a sandbox forbids, and the engine is
heavy for a call that lasts milliseconds. Or an ordinary executable, the shape
git, cargo and kubectl already use.

## Decision

A plugin is an executable named `{{ prefix }}*`, found on `PATH` or in
{{ host }}'s own plugins directory. It answers `--manifest` with JSON on stdout
and `run` with an invocation on stdin and an outcome on stdout.

It lives for the duration of one call and holds no state. It may be written in
any language; this one is written in Rust because the line is.

## Consequences

The plugin ships as its own repository with its own releases, and a host
upgrade cannot break it as long as the protocol version holds. Any language
will do. The price is a process per call, which is why the host sends the whole
subject rather than an identifier: without it, the first thing every plugin
would do is ask for the data back.
